Netlink captures used by sysdep/linux/netlink_test.c
====================================================

Each file is a pcap (link type 253, LINKTYPE_NETLINK) of one iproute2
command, captured on an nlmon interface. Every packet holds one or more
netlink messages: the request from ip(8) and the kernel's replies. The
test skips the requests (NLM_F_REQUEST) and parses the replies.

The captures come from the xtcp2 project, commit ae7b8bcb
(https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xtcp2, pkg/xtcpnl/testdata/7_3_0/dumps/),
recorded in a test VM running Linux 7.3.0 with iproute2 7.2.0. They use
documentation addresses only (RFC 5737, RFC 3849, RFC 2544).

  link-dump.pcap    netlink_route_getlink.pcap, "ip link show"
  addr-dump.pcap    netlink_route_getaddr.pcap, "ip addr show"
  route-dump.pcap   netlink_route_getroute_table_all.pcap,
                    "ip route show table all"

The VM had these interfaces:

  1 lo        loopback, mtu 65536
  2 nlmon0    nlmon, mtu 3776
  3 goip0     dummy, mtu 1500, 5e:9c:8f:0b:ce:ae
                192.0.2.1/24, 2001:db8::1/64, 2001:db8::9/64
  4 goipvrf   vrf (table 100), mtu 65575, 4a:53:32:ed:29:e2
  5 goipv     dummy, master goipvrf, mtu 1500, 86:da:71:5c:b3:da
                198.18.11.1/24

Its routes, besides the connected and local ones:

  198.18.0.0/24 via 192.0.2.10 dev goip0
  198.18.1.0/24 via 192.0.2.10 dev goip0 mtu 1400 advmss 1300
  198.18.2.0/24 via inet6 2001:db8::2 dev goip0
  198.18.3.0/24 nhid 1 via 192.0.2.10 dev goip0
  198.51.100.0/24 dev goip0 scope link
  203.0.113.0/24 nexthop via 192.0.2.10 weight 1 nexthop via 192.0.2.11 weight 3
  2001:db8:1::/64 via 2001:db8::2 dev goip0 metric 1024
  2001:db8:2::/64 nexthop via 2001:db8::2 weight 1 nexthop via 2001:db8::3 weight 3
  2001:db8:4::/64 from 2001:db8:5::/64 dev goip0

brmpls/<row>/ holds the MPLS captures, copied unchanged from xtcp2's
link-write corpus, commit 70fc28d6
(pkg/xtcpnl/testdata/7_3_0/link-write/brmpls/<row>/), same VM. Each row
runs in a fresh namespace: lo 1, nlmon0 2, then the row's devices, here a
dummy interface d0 (192.0.2.1/24, 2001:db8::1/64), with MPLS input
enabled and platform_labels 1000. After its command, each row read back
its namespace's links and addresses ("ip -d link show" and "ip addr show",
netlink_route_getlink.pcap and netlink_route_getaddr.pcap), which the
test loads first.

  mpls_route_show/ip.pcap, "ip -f mpls route show":
    100 as to 200/300 via inet 192.0.2.10 dev d0
    101 via inet 192.0.2.10 dev d0
    102 nexthop as to 201 via inet 192.0.2.10 dev d0
        nexthop as to 202 via inet 192.0.2.11 dev d0
  mpls_route_add_via6/netlink_route_getroute_mpls.pcap,
  "ip -f mpls route show" after the add:
    103 as to 203 via inet6 2001:db8::10 dev d0
  route_encap_show/ip.pcap, "ip route show dev d0":
    198.18.20.0/24 encap mpls 100/200 via 192.0.2.10
    198.18.21.0/24 nexthop encap mpls 101 via 192.0.2.10
                   nexthop encap mpls 102 via 192.0.2.11
  route_encap_show/netlink_route_getroute6.pcap, "ip -6 route show dev d0":
    2001:db8:20::/64 encap mpls 300 via 2001:db8::10
  mpls_event/ip.pcap, "ip monitor route" while 104 as to 204 and
  198.18.23.0/24 encap mpls 105 are added and deleted; here a dummy
  interface c0 comes first, so d0 is index 4

The bridge rows have a VLAN filtering bridge br0 (index 3) with one port
v0 (5), one end of a veth pair whose other end v0p (4, 02:00:00:00:00:12)
is outside the bridge:

  fdb_show/ip.pcap, "bridge fdb show br br0", after static entries for
  02:00:00:00:00:21 (VLANs 1 and 10, and none) and 02:00:00:00:00:22
  (VLAN 10), and a frame from v0p
  fdb_show/netlink_route_getneigh_bridge_all.pcap, "bridge fdb show"
  fdb_event/ip.pcap, "ip monitor all" while 02:00:00:00:00:21 is added
  and deleted, then a frame from v0p; here a dummy interface c0 comes
  first, so br0 is index 4, v0p 5 and v0 6

Netlink messages are in host byte order, so the tests of the captures
are skipped on big-endian hosts.

To look at a capture: tshark -r link-dump.pcap -V
